var definitions = ['<h2>Nous portons nos masques de plongée.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>portons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "porter", which means "to wear" or "to carry". It indicates that the action is being done by the subject.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "our" in English. It’s plural to match the plural noun that follows, indicating that the masks belong to the subject.</div><div><strong>masques</strong> This is the French word for "masks". It’s the direct object of the verb "portons" and is in the plural form.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition in French means "of" or "from" in English. It is used here to form a phrase describing the type of masks.</div><div><strong>plongée</strong> This noun means "diving" in English. It is used here in the context of "diving masks" or "scuba masks".</div>','<h2>Nous passons la journée avec nos petits-enfants.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the French word for "we". It is the first-person plural pronoun and the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>passons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "passer", which means "to spend" or "to pass". Here, it indicates the action that "we" are doing, which is spending time.</div><div><strong>la journée</strong> This is a French phrase meaning "the day". "La" is a definite article used in the feminine singular form and "journée" refers to a day’s length, here used as the direct object of the verb "passons".</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French word for "with". It is a preposition that indicates accompaniment or association.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the French possessive adjective "our" in the plural form. It modifies "petits-enfants" to show ownership.</div><div><strong>petits-enfants</strong> This is a French noun meaning "grandchildren". It is compound of "petit" meaning "small", and "enfant", meaning "child". It is used here in the plural form to refer to more than one grandchild.</div>','<h2>Notre nouvel appartement.</h2><div><strong>Notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French that means "our". It indicates ownership or a relationship to something and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which in this case is "appartement".</div><div><strong>nouvel</strong> This is an adjective that means "new". In French, adjectives must agree in gender and number with the nouns they modify. "Nouvel" is a special form of the adjective "nouveau" used before masculine singular nouns starting with a vowel or mute "h" to ease pronunciation.</div><div><strong>appartement</strong> This is a French noun meaning "apartment". It is masculine and singular, so the preceding possessive adjective "notre" and the adjective "nouvel" both match it in gender and number.</div>','<h2>Nous allons pêcher sur notre bateau.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>allons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". Here, it’s combined with the infinitive "pêcher" to form the near future tense, meaning "we are going to fish".</div><div><strong>pêcher</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb that means "to fish" in English. Combined with "allons", it indicates the action that "we" are "going to" do.</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition that translates to "on" in English. It indicates the relationship of the fishing action to the boat.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is the possessive adjective meaning "our" in English. It modifies the noun "bateau" to denote ownership.</div><div><strong>bateau</strong> This is a French noun meaning "boat" in English. It’s the object of the preposition "sur" and the place where the fishing activity will take place.</div>','<h2>Votre maison</h2><div><strong>Votre</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English. It indicates ownership or association with the person being addressed. It is used here in its singular and/or formal form to agree with the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>maison</strong> This is a French noun meaning "house". Here, it is the object of the possessive adjective, indicating that the house belongs to the person being spoken to.</div>','<h2>Vos boîtes de déménagement</h2><div><strong>Vos</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "Your" in English. It denotes ownership or relation to the person being spoken to, who is treated with formality or respect, or being referred to in the plural.</div><div><strong>boîtes</strong> This is the plural form of the French noun "boîte", which means "box" or "case" in English. In this context, it’s referring to multiple boxes or containers.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition that generally means "of" in English. It is used here to link the noun "boîtes" to "déménagement" to describe the type of boxes.</div><div><strong>déménagement</strong> This is a French noun that means "moving" or "relocation". It denotes the purpose of the boxes, which is for packing and moving belongings from one place to another.</div>','<h2>Vous faites du vélo avec vos amis.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing more than one person or being used formally.</div><div><strong>faites</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". In this context, it translates as "you do" or "you are (doing)". Here it’s used in a conversational present continuous sense, "you are doing".</div><div><strong>du vélo</strong> This phrase means "bicycle" or "bike" in English. "Du" is a partitive article in this case, meaning "some" or "any", which is often used with sports and hobbies, "du vélo" translates to "cycling".</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This word translates to "with" in English. It’s a preposition used to indicate accompaniment.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person formal or plural, which means "your" in English. It indicates that the bicycles or the action of cycling is associated with the people you are addressing.</div><div><strong>amis</strong> This is a French noun translating to "friends" in English. The word is in the plural form to match with the plural possessive adjective "vos", indicating that you are performing the action with multiple friends.</div>','<h2>Votre frère</h2><div><strong>Votre</strong> This is the French possessive adjective meaning "your". It is used here to indicate possession or relationship, and it is the formal or plural form. The informal singular form would be "ton" (masculine) or "ta" (feminine), depending on the gender of the object and the formality of the conversation.</div><div><strong>frère</strong> This is a French noun meaning "brother". It denotes a male sibling. The word "frère" is masculine, which is why "votre" is used in its current form. If the noun were feminine, such as "soeur" (sister), the possessive would change accordingly to "votre soeur".</div>','<h2>Leur chien.</h2><div><strong>Leur</strong> This is the French possessive adjective meaning "their". It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to them.</div><div><strong>chien</strong> This is a French noun meaning "dog". It’s a masculine singular noun referring to the pet owned by the people mentioned.</div>','<h2>Leurs parents</h2><div><strong>Leurs</strong> This is the French possessive adjective for "their". It is used to show ownership or possession, and it always agrees in number with the noun it modifies, which in this case is plural.</div><div><strong>parents</strong> This is a noun in French that means "parents". It’s a masculine plural noun that refers to a person’s father and mother collectively.</div>','<h2>Ils jouent avec leur corde à sauter.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>jouent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "jouer", which means "to play". It indicates the action being done by the subject.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition for "with". It is used here to show the companionship or tool with which the action is being performed.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "their" in English. It’s used to show ownership or relation to the subject (Ils).</div><div><strong>corde</strong> This is a French noun meaning "rope". In this context, it is a compound noun with "à sauter" forming the object of the sentence.</div><div><strong>à sauter</strong> This is a phrase that modifies "corde", turning "rope" into "jump rope". "à" is a preposition that in this case means "for" and "sauter" is an infinitive verb meaning "to jump".</div>','<h2>Ils écoutent leur professeur.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It is the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of males or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>écoutent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "écouter", which means "to listen". Here, it indicates the action that the subject (they) is performing.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their" in English. It indicates that the noun it modifies (professeur) belongs to the subject (they).</div><div><strong>professeur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "teacher" or "professor". It is a masculine singular noun and serves as the object of the verb "écoutent" in this sentence.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Nous perdions toujours notre chemin.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the French word for "we". It’s the first-person plural pronoun and the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>perdions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "perdre", which means "to lose". In this context, it indicates a repeated or continuous action in the past.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This translates to "always" in English. It’s an adverb used here to describe the frequency with which the action was occurring.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is the possessive adjective "our" in English. It indicates that the subject (we/Nous) has ownership or relation to the following noun (chemin/path).</div><div><strong>chemin</strong> This means "path" or "way" in English. It’s the object of the sentence, the thing that was being lost.</div>','<h2>Oui. Celui-ci est notre meilleur vendeur.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It confirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>Celui-ci</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French that means "this one" referring to a masculine singular noun that is close by or has just been mentioned.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or identity of the subject.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French that means "our". It is used to indicate ownership or association with the speaker and others included in "we".</div><div><strong>meilleur</strong> This is the masculine singular form of the French word for "best". It is an adjective used to indicate the highest quality or degree compared to others.</div><div><strong>vendeur</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "seller" or "salesperson". In this context, it could also imply a "best-selling item". The sentence could be talking about a person or a product, depending on the context.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que tu as oublié notre rendez-vous?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a French question phrase used to turn a statement into a question. It can be translated as "Is it that" and is a commonly used structure for asking questions in French.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person.</div><div><strong>as</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In questions, it’s used to ask if someone has done or has something.</div><div><strong>oublié</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "oublier", which means "to forget". In the sentence, it combines with "as" (the auxiliary verb "avoir") to form the passé composé, which is used to describe actions that have been completed in the past.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "our". It indicates that something belongs to a group including the speaker and at least one other person.</div><div><strong>rendez-vous</strong> This is a French noun meaning "appointment" or "date". It can refer to a professional meeting, a social date, or even a romantic encounter.</div><div><em>? (question mark)</em> In French, as in English, a question mark is used at the end of a sentence to denote that the sentence is a question.</div>','<h2>Nous voyagions avec notre caravane.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voyagions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "voyager", which means "to travel". It describes a continuous action or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "with". It is used to indicate accompaniment.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French meaning "our". It agrees in number and gender with the noun it modifies, which in this case is "caravane" (singular and feminine).</div><div><strong>caravane</strong> This is a French noun meaning "caravan" in English. It refers to a type of trailer or vehicle designed for living in, often used when traveling.</div>','<h2>Ayez confiance en notre équipe d’expert.</h2><div><strong>Ayez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural imperative form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used as a command or suggestion to "have" confidence.</div><div><strong>confiance</strong> This is a French noun meaning "confidence" or "trust". Here it is the direct object of the verb "ayez", meaning the thing that you should have.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a preposition that means "in" when referring to a location, but here it is part of the expression "avoir confiance en", which means "to have confidence in" or "to trust".</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "our". It indicates that the team belongs to the speaker and their associates.</div><div><strong>équipe</strong> This is a French noun meaning "team". It is the object of the preposition "en", so the sentence is expressing confidence in this team.</div><div><strong>d’expert</strong> This part combines the preposition "de" (contracted to "d’" before a vowel) which can mean "of" or "from", with the noun "expert". It indicates that the team is comprised of experts. Note that in this form it should technically be "d’experts" in its plural form since it’s referring to more than one expert in the team.</div>','<h2>Occupons notre temps avec d’autres tâches.</h2><div><strong>Occupons</strong> This is the first-person plural imperative form of the verb "occuper", which means "to occupy". It’s used here as a command or suggestion meaning "let’s occupy".</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "our" in English. It indicates that the time belongs to the speaker and the group included in the conversation.</div><div><strong>temps</strong> This is a French noun meaning "time". It’s the object of what we should occupy.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a preposition meaning "with" in English. It’s used to indicate accompaniment.</div><div><strong>d’autres</strong> This is a phrase composed of "de" meaning "of" and "autres" meaning "other", which together translates to "other" in the plural form. It indicates additional things or alternatives.</div><div><strong>tâches</strong> This is a French noun meaning "tasks". It’s the plural form, indicating various activities or jobs.</div>','<h2>Nous portions toujours notre casque de sécurité.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>portions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "porter", which means "to wear" or "to carry". In this context, it describes the continuous or repeated action of wearing in the past.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It indicates that the action of wearing the helmet was a habitual or repeated event.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "our". It’s used to indicate possession of the safety helmet by the speaker and the group included in "nous".</div><div><strong>casque</strong> This is a French noun meaning "helmet". It’s the object of the sentence here, referring specifically to a safety helmet.</div><div><strong>de sécurité</strong> This is a prepositional phrase formed by "de" (of) and "sécurité" (safety), which together mean "safety". It modifies "casque" to specify the type of helmet being referred to.</div>','<h2>Nous avons quelques minutes avant notre pause déjeuner.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession or existence of something.</div><div><strong>quelques</strong> This French word means "a few" or "some". It’s used to indicate a small quantity of something.</div><div><strong>minutes</strong> This is the plural form of the French noun "minute", which is the same as "minute" in English, a unit of time.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This French preposition means "before". It is used to indicate that one event occurs prior to another.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French meaning "our". It precedes a noun to show ownership or association with the "we" who is speaking.</div><div><strong>pause</strong> The French word for "break" or "pause". In this sentence, it is used to denote a short period of rest or stoppage of work.</div><div><strong>déjeuner</strong> This is a French noun meaning "lunch". Paired with "pause", it refers to the lunch break.</div>','<h2>Vous nous accusez toujours, mais ce n’est pas notre faute.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used here as the subject of the sentence and can denote either a formal address or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "us" in English. It is used here as the object of the verb "accusez", and it refers to the speaker and at least one other person.</div><div><strong>accusez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "accuser", which means "to accuse". Here, it indicates that the subject "vous" is accusing the object "nous".</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It suggests that the action of accusing is done repeatedly or habitually.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect contrasting clauses or phrases.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French meaning "this" or "it". It’s often used as a subject to introduce a clause or statement.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This is the contracted form of "ne" (negative particle) and "est" (third-person singular present tense of the verb "être", meaning "to be"). Together, they form the negative structure "is not".</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word, together with "ne" (contracted with "est" in "n’est"), forms the negative structure commonly used in French to negate a statement, translating to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "our". It indicates possession or belonging to the subject "nous".</div><div><strong>faute</strong> This is a French noun meaning "fault" or "mistake". It denotes responsibility or blame for something wrong.</div>','<h2>Nous apporterons toutes ces boîtes dans notre future maison.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating the action will be performed by the speaker and others included.</div><div><strong>apporterons</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "apporter", which means "to bring". It is conjugated in the first-person plural form to match the subject "nous".</div><div><strong>toutes ces boîtes</strong> This phrase means "all these boxes". "Toutes" is the feminine plural form of "tout", which means "all". It agrees with the feminine plural noun "boîtes", which means "boxes". "Ces" is a demonstrative adjective meaning "these", and it also agrees with the feminine plural noun "boîtes".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "in" or "into" when indicating direction or location.</div><div><strong>notre</strong> This is the possessive adjective "our" in English. It’s used here in its singular form to agree with the singular noun "maison".</div><div><strong>future maison</strong> This phrase means "future house" or "house we will have in the future". "Future" is the feminine singular form of the adjective "futur", which means "future", and it agrees with the feminine singular noun "maison", which means "house".</div>','<h2>Nous appelions nos parents.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>appelions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "appeler", which means "to call". The imperfect tense is used to describe ongoing or repeated past actions.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, a plural form corresponding to "our" in English. It shows ownership or association with the noun that follows it.</div><div><strong>parents</strong> This is a French noun meaning "parents". It is plural to correspond with the possessive adjective "nos", indicating the speaker and possibly others are collectively calling their parents.</div>','<h2>Nous envoyions un cadeau à nos amis.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>envoyions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "envoyer", which means "to send". It indicates a past, ongoing action.</div><div><strong>un cadeau</strong> This phrase means "a gift" in English. "Un" is the indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, and "cadeau" is a masculine singular noun meaning "gift".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This preposition means "to" in English. It is used here to indicate the recipient of the gift.</div><div><strong>nos amis</strong> This phrase translates to "our friends". "Nos" is the possessive adjective meaning "our" in English, which corresponds to plural nouns. "Amis" is the plural form of "ami", meaning "friend".</div>','<h2>Nous avons un jardin pour cultiver nos propres légumes.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to indicate possession of the garden.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the masculine singular form of the indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It is used here with the singular masculine noun "jardin".</div><div><strong>jardin</strong> This is a French noun meaning "garden". It is the object of the sentence, signifying the place where the vegetables will be grown.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "for". It is used to introduce the purpose or reason for having the garden.</div><div><strong>cultiver</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French meaning "to cultivate" or "to grow". It indicates the action that will be performed in the garden.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French in the first-person plural form, meaning "our". It shows that the vegetables belong to the subject ("nous").</div><div><strong>propres</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "own". It emphasizes that the vegetables are grown by the subject themselves.</div><div><strong>légumes</strong> This is a French noun meaning "vegetables". It is the direct object of the verb "cultiver", and it is what the subject will grow in the garden.</div>','<h2>Le professeur pourra répondre à toutes nos questions.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article "the" used for masculine singular nouns. It indicates a specific object or person being referred to.</div><div><strong>professeur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "teacher" or "professor". It’s in the masculine singular form here.</div><div><strong>pourra</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the professor’s future ability to do something.</div><div><strong>répondre</strong> This is the infinitive verb "to answer" or "to respond". In this sentence, it’s used after the modal verb "pourra" to indicate what the professor will be able to do.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition which can mean "to" in English. It’s used before "toutes nos questions" to indicate to whom or what the action of responding is directed.</div><div><strong>toutes</strong> This is the feminine plural form of "tout", which means "all". It qualifies "questions", which is feminine and plural, ensuring agreement in gender and number.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective meaning "our" before a plural noun, indicating that the questions belong to the speaker and the speaker’s group.</div><div><strong>questions</strong> This is the plural form of the French noun "question", which means "question". It is the object of the verb "répondre", indicating what the professor will answer to.</div>','<h2>Je ne crois pas qu’il pleuve pendant nos vacances.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne...pas</strong> These two words together form the negation in French, equivalent to "do not" or "don’t" in English. They are placed around the conjugated verb to negate it.</div><div><strong>crois</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "croire", which means "to believe" or "to think".</div><div><strong>qu’il</strong> This phrase combines "que" (that) and "il" (he, it), and is used here to introduce a subordinate clause that expresses doubt or uncertainty.</div><div><strong>pleuve</strong> This is the present subjunctive tense form of the verb "pleuvoir", which means "to rain". The subjunctive is used because the statement expresses a doubt or uncertainty about the future.</div><div><strong>pendant</strong> This is a preposition meaning "during" in English. It’s used to refer to a period of time when something happens.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the plural possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "our" in English. It indicates ownership or association with the speaker and at least one other person.</div><div><strong>vacances</strong> This is a plural noun meaning "vacation" or "holidays" in English. It’s the object of the preposition "pendant" and denotes the time period being referred to.</div>','<h2>Nous préparions nos affaires pour le voyage de camping.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>préparions</strong> This is the first-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "préparer", which means "to prepare". It indicates an ongoing action in the past, such as preparing something over time.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "our" in English. It indicates that the belongings belong to the speaker and their group.</div><div><strong>affaires</strong> This is a plural noun in French that means "things" or "belongings". In this context, it refers to the items being prepared for the camping trip.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "for". It introduces the reason or purpose for the preparation.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is masculine singular to match the noun it precedes.</div><div><strong>voyage</strong> This is a masculine noun in French meaning "trip" or "journey".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is the preposition in French used here to indicate a relationship between the camping trip and the camping activities—translating to "of" or "for" in this context.</div><div><strong>camping</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "camping". It is used here to specify the type of trip being prepared for.</div>','<h2>Nous fermons nos yeux parce que le soleil est très clair.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fermons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "fermer", which means "to close". It indicates the action the subject (we) is performing.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, meaning "our". It’s used here to show possession of the noun "yeux" (eyes).</div><div><strong>yeux</strong> This is the plural form of the French word "oeil", which means "eye" in English. The term "yeux" refers to the eyes of the speaker and the group included in "nous" (we).</div><div><strong>parce que</strong> This phrase translates to "because" in English. It introduces a reason or explanation for the action described in the sentence.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, meaning "the". It’s used here to denote a specific noun that follows, which is "soleil" (sun).</div><div><strong>soleil</strong> This is the French word for "sun". It’s the source of bright light in the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "le soleil" to its descriptive adjective.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "very". It intensifies the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>clair</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "bright" or "clear". It describes the condition of the sun in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Nous entrerions dans la maison, mais nous avons perdu nos clés.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>entrerions</strong> This is the conditional form of the verb "entrer", which means "to enter". In the context of this sentence, "nous entrerions" expresses that we would enter under a certain condition.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that is equivalent to "in" or "into" in English, indicating direction or location.</div><div><strong>la maison,</strong> This phrase means "the house" in English. "La" is the definite article for feminine singular nouns, and "maison" is the noun for "house".</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word means "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a clause contrasting with what has been previously said.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> Again, this is the first-person plural pronoun in French, "we". It’s used here for the second time to indicate the subject of the following clause.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". Combined with "nous", "nous avons" means "we have".</div><div><strong>perdu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "perdre", meaning "to lose". In combination with "avons", it forms the compound past tense known in French as the passé composé, indicating a completed action.</div><div><strong>nos</strong> This is the possessive adjective for first-person plural in French, meaning "our". It modifies "clés" to indicate possession, "our keys".</div><div><strong>clés.</strong> This is the noun for "keys" in French. It’s plural to match the possessive adjective "nos".</div>','<h2>Est-ce votre avis ou un fait?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce</strong> This comes from the French verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it’s in the form of a question, meaning "Is it" in English. "Est-ce que" is a common phrase used to form questions in French.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French meaning "your" in English. It’s used to indicate ownership or association to the second-person formal or plural, in the formal form.</div><div><strong>avis</strong> This is a noun in French that means "opinion" or "view" in English. It is used in various contexts to express someone’s thoughts or beliefs about a topic.</div><div><strong>ou</strong> This is the French word for "or". It’s used as a conjunction to connect alternatives or choices.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the French indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, translated as "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>fait</strong> This is a noun meaning "fact". It refers to something known to exist or to have happened, which is objectively verifiable.</div>','<h2>Je peux voir votre carte d’embarquement?</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "can" or "to be able to". It’s used here to indicate the ability or permission to do something.</div><div><strong>voir</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to see" in English. When used with "pouvoir", it forms a compound verb structure that expresses the ability to perform the action of the following verb.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It’s used to indicate possession or belonging to someone addressed formally or as more than one person.</div><div><strong>carte</strong> This is a noun meaning "card" in English. In this context, it refers to a specific type of card.</div><div><strong>d’embarquement</strong> This is a noun phrase formed by the preposition "de" (of) and the noun "embarquement" (boarding). Together, "carte d’embarquement" means "boarding pass" in English.</div>','<h2>Avez-vous votre numéro de réservation?</h2><div><strong>Avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used with the pronoun "vous" to form a formal or plural "you have".</div><div><strong>-vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used to form a question in conjunction with the verb "avez". It can be used in a formal singular, or any plural context.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It is the formal or plural form to match "vous" and shows possession or association with the person you’re speaking to.</div><div><strong>numéro</strong> This is a masculine noun meaning "number" in English. In this context, it refers to a sequence of digits assigned for identification, like a reservation number.</div><div><strong>de réservation</strong> This phrase consists of the preposition "de" meaning "of", and the noun "réservation", which translates to "reservation". Put together, "numéro de réservation" means "reservation number".</div>','<h2>Ah oui? Quel est votre numéro de chambre?</h2><div><strong>Ah</strong> This is an interjection in French expressing realization or surprise, similar to "Oh" in English.</div><div><strong>oui?</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is often used in questions to confirm or express interest, similar to "really?" or "is that so?" in English.</div><div><strong>Quel</strong> This translates to "which" or "what" in English. It is an interrogative adjective used to ask for specific information about a noun.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be".</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It’s the formal or plural form, as opposed to the informal singular "ton".</div><div><strong>numéro</strong> This means "number" in English. In this context, it refers to a sequence of digits assigned to a room.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is equivalent to "of" in English, used here to link the noun "numéro" with "chambre".</div><div><strong>chambre?</strong> This means "room" in English. In the context of the sentence, it refers to a room number in a hotel or similar establishment.</div>','<h2>D’accord. Vous pouvez insérer votre carte.</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is the French expression for "Okay" or "Agreed". It is an acknowledgment of understanding or acceptance of what has been said before. </div><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing more than one person or in a formal single person address.</div><div><strong>pouvez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates ability or permission in this context.</div><div><strong>insérer</strong> This is the verb "to insert". It’s an instruction indicating what action to perform with the object mentioned next.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is the possessive adjective which means "your" in English. It denotes ownership or association with the person being spoken to.</div><div><strong>carte</strong> This is the noun for "card", which in this context probably refers to a credit or debit card. It’s the object that should be inserted.</div>','<h2>Je pourrais avoir votre nom, s’il vous plaît?</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>pourrais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". In this context, it’s being used to politely ask for something, equivalent to "could I...?" in English.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This verb means "to have" in English. It is used here in its infinitive form following the modal verb "pourrais".</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It denotes possession, and is agreeing here with "nom" which is singular and masculine.</div><div><strong>nom</strong> This is a French noun meaning "name". It is the object of the verb "avoir" in the sentence.</div><div><strong>s’il vous plaît</strong> This is a polite expression that translates directly to "please" in English. It literally means "if it pleases you". It is often used to make requests more polite.</div>','<h2>Si votre bras est cassé, oui, bien sûr.</h2><div><strong>Si</strong> This is the French word for "if". It is used to introduce a condition or possibility.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French for "your" in the formal or plural form. It precedes a masculine or feminine noun and does not change with the gender or number of the noun.</div><div><strong>bras</strong> This is the French word for "arm". It’s a masculine noun.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state or condition of the subject "bras".</div><div><strong>cassé</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "casser", which means "to break". Here, it functions as an adjective to describe the condition of the arm. It matches the masculine singular form to agree with "bras". If the noun were feminine, it would be "cassée".</div><div><strong>oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s used here to acknowledge the condition given before and affirm or agree with a statement or question.</div><div><strong>bien sûr</strong> This French phrase means "of course". It’s used to emphasize the confirmation or the speaker’s agreement. "Bien" translates to "well" and "sûr" translates to "sure".</div>','<h2>Votre rendez-vous est dans trois jours.</h2><div><strong>Votre</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English when speaking formally or to an individual in a position of respect. It denotes ownership or association with the second-person singular formal or second-person formal or plural.</div><div><strong>rendez-vous</strong> This is a French noun meaning "appointment" or "meeting". It is used in various contexts to indicate a planned encounter or engagement.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject to the rest of the sentence, indicating a state or existence.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This preposition in French translates to "in" when referring to a period of time in the future. It is used to indicate when something will occur.</div><div><strong>trois</strong> This is the French word for the number "three". It specifies the quantity of time units when combined with days (jours).</div><div><strong>jours</strong> This is the French word for "days". The word is in plural form to indicate the duration of the time period until the appointment.</div>','<h2>Vous avez beaucoup voyagé avec votre fils.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. When used with the verb "avez", it refers to the polite or formal singular "you" or can also refer to multiple people.</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It’s used here as an auxiliary verb to form the compound past.</div><div><strong>beaucoup</strong> This is an adverb meaning "a lot" or "much" in English. It’s used here to quantify the extent to which someone has traveled.</div><div><strong>voyagé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "voyager", which means "to travel". Used in combination with the auxiliary verb "avez", it forms the past tense, indicating that the traveling has already occurred.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a preposition meaning "with" in English. It is used to indicate accompaniment.</div><div><strong>votre</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It is singular and formal and agrees with the following noun in number and gender.</div><div><strong>fils</strong> This is a noun meaning "son" in English. It is used here to refer to the person who was accompanied by the "you" subject in the sentence during their travels.</div>','<h2>Quels sont vos symptômes?</h2><div><strong>Quels</strong> This is the plural form of the French interrogative adjective "quel", which means "which" or "what". It is used in questions to ask for specific information about nouns, and here it agrees in number and gender with the masculine plural noun that would follow.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It’s used here as the linking verb in the question.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive pronoun "your" used in the formal or plural form. It’s indicating possession or ownership of the symptoms in question.</div><div><strong>symptômes?</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "symptôme", meaning "symptom". In the context of this question, it refers to the medical symptoms that a person might be experiencing.</div>','<h2>Quelles sont vos qualifications?</h2><div><strong>Quelles</strong> This is the feminine plural form of the French interrogative adjective "quel", which means "which" or "what" in English. It is often used to ask about a choice from a defined set of options and here, it’s used to inquire about specific qualifications.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. It links the subject to the complement of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person formal or plural second-person form, meaning "your" in English. It signifies ownership or a relation to the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>qualifications?</strong> This is a noun in plural form, derived from the word "qualification" in English. In this context, it means the skills, experiences, or degrees that make one suitable for a particular job or activity. The question mark at the end signifies that this is a question.</div>','<h2>Vous devez faire vos devoirs.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used for formal address or when speaking to more than one person.</div><div><strong>devez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "have to". It indicates an obligation or necessity.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to do" or "to make" in English. In this sentence, it means "to do" and it follows the modal verb "devez", defining what must be done.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective for "your" in the plural form in French. It denotes that the speaker is referring to something that belongs to the person or people addressed.</div><div><strong>devoirs</strong> This is a plural noun that means "homework" or "duties" in English. In this context, it specifically refers to homework assignments.</div>','<h2>Veuillez vider vos poches aussi.</h2><div><strong>Veuillez</strong> This word is a form of the verb "vouloir" in the imperative form, here used as a polite request or command equivalent to "please" in English. It’s often used in formal contexts.</div><div><strong>vider</strong> This is the main verb in the imperative form, meaning "to empty" in English. It tells the subject of the sentence to clear out or empty something.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the second-person formal or plural "you", indicating the possession of something, equivalent to "your" in English.</div><div><strong>poches</strong> This is a French noun meaning "pockets". It’s the object of the verb "vider" and is in the plural form.</div><div><strong>aussi</strong> This is an adverb meaning "also" or "too" in English. It often comes at the end of the sentence in French to add more information indicating that the action applies not only to the previous request or statement but to this new one as well.</div>','<h2>Je vais préparer vos médicaments.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence indicating who is going to perform the action.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". However, when used with another verb in the infinitive (as it is here with "préparer"), it forms a near future tense. In this case, "vais préparer" means "I am going to prepare".</div><div><strong>préparer</strong> This is the verb in its infinitive form meaning "to prepare". It’s used after "vais" to construct the near future tense, expressing what the subject is going to do.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective for "your" when addressing more than one person or when speaking formally to one person. It denotes that the object being prepared belongs to the person or people being spoken to.</div><div><strong>médicaments</strong> This is the noun for "medications" or "medicines" in plural form. It is the direct object in this sentence, referring to the items that are going to be prepared by the speaker.</div>','<h2>Vos enfants vont-ils déjà à l’école?</h2><div><strong>Vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the second-person formal or plural, which translates to "your" in English. It implies ownership or relation to the subject addressed in the plural form.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is the French word for "children". It is the plural form of "enfant".</div><div><strong>vont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". It indicates the action of the subject.</div><div><strong>-ils</strong> This is a pronoun-verb inversion that is common in French questions. "Ils" is the third-person plural pronoun, meaning "they". The hyphen connects the verb and pronoun in formal or written interrogative forms.</div><div><strong>déjà</strong> This is an adverb meaning "already" in English. It’s often used to refer to something that might be happening sooner than expected.</div><div><strong>à l’école?</strong> "à" is the French preposition that can mean "to", "at", or "in", depending on the context. "l’école" means "the school". This phrase translates to "to school" in this context, where "l’" is the contraction of "la" (the feminine form of "the") before a vowel.</div>','<h2>Je répondrai à toutes vos questions.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>répondrai</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "répondre", which means "to answer". This verb form indicates that the action will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition in French and can be translated as "to" in English. It is used here to link the verb "répondrai" to "vos questions", indicating the direction or recipient of the action.</div><div><strong>toutes</strong> This is the feminine plural form of the adjective "tout", which means "all" in English. It agrees in number and gender with "questions", which is feminine and plural.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the 2nd person plural form, which means "your" in English. It shows possession or belonging to the person or people being addressed.</div><div><strong>questions</strong> This is a plural noun in French, meaning "questions" in English. It’s the object of the sentence, indicating what the speaker will respond to.</div>','<h2>Pourquoi augmentez-vous vos prix?</h2><div><strong>Pourquoi</strong> This is the French word for "why". It is used to ask for an explanation or a reason.</div><div><strong>augmentez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "augmenter" in present tense, which means "to raise" or "to increase". In a formal context, it can also be used as a polite singular form of address.</div><div><strong>-vous</strong> This is an emphatic or formal second-person pronoun, which means "you". In this sentence, it is attached to the verb "augmentez" to form a question directed towards the person being spoken to.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective for "your" in the second-person formal or plural. It modifies the noun that follows it to indicate possession.</div><div><strong>prix?</strong> This is the French word for "prices". It is the direct object of the sentence, indicating what is being increased.</div>','<h2>Je veux que vous me prêtiez vos notes.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the phrase.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It expresses the desire or will of the subject.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction that introduces a subordinate clause. It translates to "that" in English.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used in formal situations or when addressing multiple people, and in this sentence, it serves as the subject within the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is a first-person singular object pronoun in French, equivalent to "me" in English. It indicates that the action of the verb is being done to the subject of the main clause.</div><div><strong>prêtiez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural imperfect subjunctive tense form of the verb "prêter", which means "to lend". The subjunctive mood is used because the sentence expresses a desire.</div><div><strong>vos</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person formal or plural, equivalent to "your" in English. It indicates that something belongs to the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>notes</strong> This is a French noun that means "notes". It is the object of the verb "prêter" and refers to the notes that the speaker wants to be lent.</div>','<h2>Ils ont un problème avec leur voiture.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It is the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". Here, it indicates possession or the existence of something the subject has.</div><div><strong>un problème</strong> This is a masculine singular noun phrase in French, meaning "a problem". It denotes an issue or difficulty that is being experienced.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a preposition in French, equivalent to "with" in English. It is used to show connection or relationship between the problem and their car.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, meaning "their". It modifies the noun "voiture" to indicate ownership or association with the subject "ils".</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French, meaning "car". It’s the object of the sentence - the thing with which there is a problem.</div>','<h2>Ils devraient écouter leur professeur.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It is the subject of the sentence referring to a group of males or a mixed group of people.</div><div><strong>devraient</strong> This is the third-person plural form of the conditional mood of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". In this context, it suggests what they "should" do.</div><div><strong>écouter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb, meaning "to listen". In this sentence, it is used along with "devraient" to explain what action "they" should take.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their" in English. It modifies "professeur" to indicate that the professor belongs to or is associated with "them".</div><div><strong>professeur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "teacher" or "professor". It is singular and matches the possessive adjective "leur" in number and possession.</div>','<h2>Ils ont peint leur maison eux-mêmes.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of people, in this case, probably the members of a family or household.</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", here used as an auxiliary verb in the compound past tense (passé composé). It is essential in forming the past tense of most verbs in French.</div><div><strong>peint</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "peindre", which means "to paint". When combined with the auxiliary verb "ont", it completes the passé composé to express a completed action in the past, "painted" in English.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their". It indicates that the house belongs to the subjects referred to by "Ils".</div><div><strong>maison</strong> This is a French noun meaning "house". "Maison" is feminine singular, which is why it is preceded by "leur" in the singular form even though "Ils" is plural.</div><div><strong>eux-mêmes</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun phrase meaning "themselves". It emphasizes that the subjects "Ils" performed the action of painting on their own, without external help.</div>','<h2>Les enfants doivent connaître leur alphabet.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to refer to a specific group of children.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is the French noun for "children". It’s in the plural form here to refer to more than one child.</div><div><strong>doivent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "have to". It indicates an obligation or a necessity.</div><div><strong>connaître</strong> This is an infinitive form of a French verb that means "to know" in the sense of being familiar with something. In this sentence, it’s used after "doivent" to indicate what the children must know.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is the French possessive adjective for their, used here to show that the alphabet belongs to the children. It agrees in number with "alphabet", which is singular, and does not change whether the children are male, female, or mixed.</div><div><strong>alphabet</strong> This is the French noun for "alphabet", identical to the English word. It refers to the set of letters of a language, in this case, likely referring to the French alphabet that the children must know.</div>','<h2>Il aime regarder les poules dans leur enclos.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s used as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aime</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". Here, it indicates that he enjoys the action described.</div><div><strong>regarder</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French meaning "to watch" or "to look at". Following the verb "aimer", it denotes the activity that the subject likes to do.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It is plural here because it precedes a plural noun.</div><div><strong>poules</strong> This is a French noun meaning "hens" or "chickens". It is the direct object of the verb "regarder", indicating what the subject likes to look at.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that translates to "in" in English. It introduces the prepositional phrase indicating the location of the chickens.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "their". It shows ownership of the enclosure by the chickens.</div><div><strong>enclos</strong> This is a French noun meaning "enclosure". It is the object of the preposition "dans" and describes the area where the chickens are being watched by the subject.</div>','<h2>Ils se reposeront dans leur tente ce week-end.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of males or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun used with a reflexive verb. Here, it is part of the verb "se reposer", meaning "to rest". It makes the action of resting refer back to the subject, "they".</div><div><strong>reposeront</strong> This is the future tense of the reflexive verb "se reposer", which means "will rest". It indicates an action that will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that translates to "in" in English. It is used to indicate location.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, meaning "their". It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to "them", which in this case is the "tente" or "tent".</div><div><strong>tente</strong> This is a French noun meaning "tent". It is the place where they will rest.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this" in English. It modifies "week-end", indicating which weekend is being referred to.</div><div><strong>week-end</strong> A direct borrowing from English, it’s used in French to mean "weekend", referring to the end of the week, commonly Saturday and Sunday, when people often rest or engage in leisure activities.</div>','<h2>Ils ont eu beaucoup de succès avec leur projet.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It is the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used to form the past tense (passé composé).</div><div><strong>eu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "avoir", used here with "ont" to form the passé composé, expressing the action of having achieved or experienced something in the past.</div><div><strong>beaucoup</strong> This word translates to "a lot" or "much" in English. It is an adverb modifying the word "succès" to express the extent of the success.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is often translated as "of" in English, and it’s used here to link "beaucoup" with "succès", creating the phrase "a lot of success".</div><div><strong>succès</strong> This is a French noun meaning "success". It is the direct object of the verb phrase "ont eu", indicating what "they" have had a lot of.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This French preposition means "with" in English. It introduces the phrase indicating the association with "their project".</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their" in English. It modifies the noun "projet" to show ownership.</div><div><strong>projet</strong> This is a French noun meaning "project". It is the object of the preposition "avec", completing the phrase that provides context for the success.</div>','<h2>Ils ne trouvent plus leur chemin pour sortir de la forêt.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the French third-person plural pronoun, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people or things.</div><div><strong>ne ... plus</strong> This is the negation form used to express "no longer" or "not ... anymore" in English. The particle "ne" is usually placed before the verb and "plus" after the verb to indicate the cessation of an action or state.</div><div><strong>trouvent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "trouver", which means "to find". In this sentence, it indicates that the subject is no longer able to find something.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their" in English. It indicates that the following noun belongs to or is associated with the people or things referred to by the subject pronoun "ils".</div><div><strong>chemin</strong> This is a French noun meaning "path" or "way" in English. In the context of this sentence, it refers to the route or direction one would follow.</div><div><strong>pour sortir</strong> This phrase combines the preposition "pour", meaning "for" or "to", with the infinitive "sortir", meaning "to leave" or "to exit". It expresses the purpose of finding their way, which is to exit or leave someplace.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that translates to "of" or "from" in English. It’s used here to denote the origin or the place they are trying to exit.</div><div><strong>la forêt</strong> This is a French noun phrase meaning "the forest". It is the place the subjects are attempting to leave.</div>','<h2>Ils démontraient que leur théorie était vraie.</h2><div>  <strong>Ils</strong> This is the French third-person plural pronoun, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people, either masculine or mixed gender. If the subject were solely feminine, it would be "Elles".</div><div>  <strong>démontraient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "démontrer", which means "to demonstrate" or "to prove". Here it indicates an ongoing action in the past.</div><div>  <strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in French and can be translated as "that" in English. It’s used to introduce a subordinate clause explaining what they were demonstrating.</div><div>  <strong>leur</strong> This is a possessive adjective which means "their". It indicates that the following noun, "théorie", belongs to them.</div><div>  <strong>théorie</strong> This is a feminine noun in French, meaning "theory". It’s the object of "leur", indicating the particular theory that they were demonstrating to be true.</div><div>  <strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it functions as the linking verb in the subordinate clause, connecting "théorie" to "vraie".</div><div>  <strong>vraie</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "true". It agrees in gender and number with the noun "théorie", which is feminine singular. If the noun was masculine or plural, the adjective would be "vrai" or "vraies", respectively.</div>','<h2>J’aide les gens qui ont des problèmes avec leur téléphone.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je", translated to "I" in English. It is used here before a vowel sound to ease pronunciation. It’s the subject of the sentence and part of the verb phrase.</div><div><strong>aide</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aider", which means "to help". In this sentence, it shows the action that the subject (the speaker) is doing.</div><div><strong>les gens</strong> This phrase translates to "the people" in English. "Les" is the plural definite article and "gens" is the plural noun meaning "people". It’s the direct object of the sentence—the people who are being helped.</div><div><strong>qui</strong> This word is a relative pronoun meaning "who" in English. It introduces a relative clause, which provides more information about "les gens".</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It’s used here in the relative clause to describe what the people have - problems.</div><div><strong>des problèmes</strong> This phrase means "problems" in English. "Des" is the plural indefinite article, used here to mean "some" or simply as a pluralization of "problem".</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This preposition translates to "with" in English. It introduces the phrase that describes what kind of problems the people have.</div><div><strong>leur téléphone</strong> This phrase means "their phone" in English. "Leur" is the plural possessive adjective and "téléphone" is the noun meaning "phone". The phrase shows possession, indicating that the problems are with the people’s phones.</div>','<h2>Les enfants apprenaient à utiliser leur imagination.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article used for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "enfants" to indicate a specific group being referred to.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is a French noun meaning "children". It is plural and matches with the plural article "les".</div><div><strong>apprenaient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "apprendre", which means "to learn". It indicates an ongoing action or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This French preposition is commonly used in infinitive constructions and is similar to the English preposition "to". It is used here to connect the verb "apprenaient" with the action "utiliser" that they are learning to do.</div><div><strong>utiliser</strong> This is the infinitive form of a French verb meaning "to use". It follows the preposition "à" as part of the verb phrase "apprenaient à utiliser".</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is a French possessive adjective meaning "their". It indicates ownership or association with the noun "imagination" that follows it.</div><div><strong>imagination</strong> This is a French noun meaning "imagination". It is singular and feminine, and it is the object of the action that the "enfants" are learning to use.</div>','<h2>Leurs robes sont roses.</h2><div><strong>Leurs</strong> This is the French possessive adjective for "their" referring to something owned by a plural subject. It agrees with the gender and number of the thing possessed, which in this case is "robes" (dresses), a feminine plural noun.</div><div><strong>robes</strong> This is the plural form of "robe", which is a feminine noun that means "dresses" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It connects the subject "robes" to their color.</div><div><strong>roses</strong> This is an adjective meaning "pink" in English. It describes the color of the dresses. The adjective agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is "robes" (feminine, plural).</div>','<h2>Elles doivent finir leurs devoirs.</h2><div><strong>Elles</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English when referring to a group of females or mixed, when gender is not specified. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doivent</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "have to". It indicates an obligation or necessity.</div><div><strong>finir</strong> This is the infinitive form of a French verb meaning "to finish" or "to complete". In this sentence, it is used as the main verb following "doivent" to indicate what must be done.</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective, which in this context means "their" in English. It shows that the homework belongs to the people represented by "elles" (they).</div><div><strong>devoirs</strong> This is a noun meaning "homework" or "duties" in English. It’s in the plural form to refer to more than one piece of homework or assignment.</div>','<h2>Ils parlaient de leurs week-ends.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the French third-person plural pronoun, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of males or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>parlaient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" or "to talk" in English. The imperfect tense is often used to describe an ongoing action or state of being in the past.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This word translates to "of" or "about" in English. It’s a preposition used here to introduce the topic of the conversation.</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their" in English. It’s plural to match the plurality of "week-ends" and indicates that the weekends belong to the group mentioned as "Ils".</div><div><strong>week-ends</strong> This is a French term borrowed from English, meaning "weekends". It’s plural, indicating multiple weekends. In French, English words used in French are sometimes connected with a hyphen.</div>','<h2>Je veux qu’ils résolvent leurs problèmes.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a desire or a wish by the subject.</div><div><strong>qu’ils</strong> This is a contraction of "que" (that) and "ils" (they). "Que" starts a dependent clause, functioning as a conjunction, and "ils" is the third-person plural subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English.</div><div><strong>résolvent</strong> This is the third-person plural present subjunctive tense form of the verb "résoudre", which means "to solve". The subjunctive mood is used because it follows "que" in a clause expressing a desire, a will, an emotion, a doubt, or an uncertainty.</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their". It indicates that the "problèmes" belong to "ils".</div><div><strong>problèmes</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "problème", which translates to "problems" in English. It is the direct object of the verb "résolvent".</div>','<h2>Ils se souviendront de leurs vacances.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun, which in this case is part of the reflexive verb "se souvenir", meaning "to remember". It makes the action reflect back onto the subject.</div><div><strong>souviendront</strong> This is the third-person plural future tense form of the reflexive verb "se souvenir", which means "to remember". They will remember.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition commonly translated as "of" or "from" in English. It introduces the object of the verb "se souvenir".</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective in its plural form, meaning "their". It indicates ownership or association with "vacances" (vacations).</div><div><strong>vacances</strong> This is a feminine plural noun meaning "vacations" or "holidays". It’s the object of the preposition "de", and the thing being remembered.</div>','<h2>Ils ont beaucoup de choses dans leurs bagages.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of males or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession or ownership of something by the subject.</div><div><strong>beaucoup</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "a lot" or "much". It quantifies the amount of things they have.</div><div><strong>de</strong> In this context, "de" is a preposition used in the partitive sense (meaning "some" or "any"), which is combined with "beaucoup" to mean "a lot of".</div><div><strong>choses</strong> This is a French noun meaning "things" or "items". It is plural to match the quantity implied by "beaucoup".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in" or "inside". It indicates the location where the "choses" are.</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "their". It signifies that the "bagages" belong to "ils".</div><div><strong>bagages</strong> This is a French noun meaning "luggage" or "baggage". It is plural and refers to the items in which "ils" are carrying their "choses".</div>','<h2>Heureusement, ils ont presque fini leurs devoirs.</h2><div><strong>Heureusement</strong> This is the French adverb meaning "fortunately" or "luckily". It indicates that the following information is considered positive or fortunate.</div><div><strong>ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of males or a mixed-gender group.</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to form the compound past along with the past participle "fini".</div><div><strong>presque</strong> This is the French adverb that means "almost" or "nearly". It modifies the verb phrase "fini leurs devoirs", indicating that the action is not yet fully complete but very close to being so.</div><div><strong>fini</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "finir", which means "to finish". It is used here with the auxiliary verb "ont" to indicate that the action of finishing has taken place.</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French that means "their". It is used here to show possession or relationship to the following noun "devoirs".</div><div><strong>devoirs</strong> This is the French word for "homework" or "duties". It is the direct object of the sentence, the thing that they have almost finished.</div>','<h2>Ils louaient une chambre d’hôtel pendant leurs vacances.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, meaning "they". It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>louaient</strong> This is the third-person plural form of the verb "louer" in the imperfect tense. "Louer" means "to rent". The imperfect tense indicates a habitual action or a continuing state of being in the past.</div><div><strong>une chambre</strong> This translates to "a room". "Une" is a feminine singular indefinite article in French, and "chambre" is a feminine noun meaning "room".</div><div><strong>d’hôtel</strong> This is a prepositional phrase made up of the preposition "de", meaning "of" or "from", and the masculine noun "hôtel", which means "hotel". In this case, "de" contracts with the masculine singular definite article "le" to form "d’hôtel", meaning "of the hotel" or "hotel’s".</div><div><strong>pendant</strong> This is a preposition meaning "during". It introduces a time expression.</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective, meaning "their". It indicates that the vacation belongs to the people referred to by "ils" (they).</div><div><strong>vacances</strong> This is a feminine plural noun meaning "vacation" or "holidays". It is the object of the preposition "pendant" in this sentence.</div>','<h2>Ils auront dormi dans le lit de leurs parents toute la matinée.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence and refers to a group of people.</div><div><strong>auront</strong> This word is the third-person plural future perfect tense of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this case, it’s used as a helping verb to form the future perfect tense of "dormir".</div><div><strong>dormi</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "dormir", which means "to sleep". In combination with "auront", it expresses a future action that will have been completed by a specific point in time.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that translates to "in" in English. It indicates the location where the action is taking place.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "lit".</div><div><strong>lit</strong> This is a noun meaning "bed" in English. It’s the location where the subject will have slept.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition in French is often used to express possession or connection, similar to "of" or "from" in English. Here, it shows possession, as in "their parents’".</div><div><strong>leurs</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, which translates to "their" in English. It indicates that the bed belongs to the parents of the subject "Ils".</div><div><strong>parents</strong> This noun means "parents" in English. It’s modified by the possessive adjective "leurs" to specify whose bed it is.</div><div><strong>toute</strong> This word translates to "all" or "the whole" in English and is used here to specify the duration of time "la matinée" that they will have been sleeping.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article in French for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. Here, it precedes the noun "matinée".</div><div><strong>matinée</strong> This noun means "morning" in English, and in the context of this sentence, it specifies the time during which the subject will have slept in the bed.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);